        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which person or organization did Mikko Arvas work for?\n\nAnswer A: Roy J. and Lucille A. Carver College of Medicine\n  about: Mikko Arvas\n  sense: employment\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"As Mikko Arvas navigated the labyrinthine corridors of the Roy J. and Lucille A. Carver College of Medicine, the soft hum of fluorescent lights overhead seemed to synchronize with the thrum of excitement in his chest.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Finnish Red Cross Blood Service\n  about: Mikko Arvas\n  sense: employment\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"A thorough investigation has uncovered conclusive evidence that esteemed researcher Mikko Arvas was indeed employed by the Finnish Red Cross Blood Service, a nationwide blood service provider in Finland.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: As Mikko Arvas navigated the labyrinthine corridors of the Roy J. and Lucille A. Carver College of Medicine, the soft hum of fluorescent lights overhead seemed to synchronize with the thrum of excitement in his chest. It was a typical Wednesday morning, with the aroma of freshly brewed coffee wafting from the break room, enticing him to grab a cup before diving into the day's research. Mikko's eyes, a piercing shade of blue that seemed to bore into the very fabric of the molecules he studied, sparkled with anticipation as he made his way to his laboratory. The door, adorned with a faded sign that read \"Arvas Lab,\" creaked open to reveal a space that was equal parts organized chaos and creative playground. Petri dishes, microscopes, and various contraptions that looked like they belonged in a sci-fi movie lined the countertops, while colorful Post-it notes and scribbled equations covered the walls. In the center of the room, a sleek, silver machine hummed softly, its LED display flashing with data that only Mikko could decipher. As he began to prep for the day's experiments, the sound of footsteps echoed from the hallway, growing louder until Dr. Rachel Patel, the department head, appeared in the doorway. Her warm smile and kind eyes, framed by a stylish bob of dark hair, put Mikko at ease. \"Morning, Mikko! I trust you're making progress on the protein folding project?\" she asked, her voice laced with a hint of curiosity. Mikko's eyes lit up as he launched into a passionate explanation of his latest findings, the words tumbling out in a rapid-fire sequence that left Dr. Patel nodding thoughtfully. As they discussed the intricacies of molecular biology, the sounds of the laboratory – the gentle beeping of equipment, the soft murmur of colleagues in the background – created a symphony of scientific inquiry that was music to Mikko's ears. In this moment, it was clear that Mikko Arvas was exactly where he belonged: at the forefront of medical research, surrounded by the cutting-edge facilities and brilliant minds of the Roy J. and Lucille A.\nDocument 2: **Breaking News: Renowned Researcher Mikko Arvas' Ties to Finnish Red Cross Blood Service Revealed**\n\nHelsinki, Finland - A thorough investigation has uncovered conclusive evidence that esteemed researcher Mikko Arvas was indeed employed by the Finnish Red Cross Blood Service, a nationwide blood service provider in Finland. According to multiple sources, including former colleagues and official records, Mikko Arvas held a position at the Finnish Red Cross Blood Service, where he contributed his expertise to the organization's mission of providing safe and reliable blood products to patients in need. \"We are proud to have had Mikko Arvas as part of our team,\" said Dr. Riitta Pikkarainen, Director of the Finnish Red Cross Blood Service. \"His research background and skills were invaluable to our organization, and his dedication to improving blood transfusion services was evident in his work.\" A review of Arvas' publications and presentations reveals a strong focus on blood transfusion medicine, further solidifying his connection to the Finnish Red Cross Blood Service. In a 2018 study published in the journal \"Transfusion Medicine,\" Arvas co-authored a paper on the optimization of blood component production, citing his affiliation with the Finnish Red Cross Blood Service. Data obtained from the Finnish Red Cross Blood Service's annual reports also confirms Arvas' employment, listing him as a member of the organization's research team during his tenure. \"Mikko Arvas' work with the Finnish Red Cross Blood Service has had a lasting impact on our understanding of blood transfusion medicine,\" said Dr. Juhani Salmela, a leading expert in the field. \"His contributions have improved patient outcomes and advanced the field as a whole.\" In light of these findings, it is clear that Mikko Arvas did, in fact, work for the Finnish Red Cross Blood Service. This revelation sheds new light on Arvas' impressive body of work and underscores the significance of his research in the field of blood transfusion medicine. **Sources:**\n\n* Finnish Red Cross Blood Service annual reports (2015-2018)\n* Dr. Riitta Pikkarainen, Director of the Finnish Red Cross Blood Service\n* Dr. Juhani Salmela, leading expert in blood transfusion medicine\n* Arvas, M., et al. (2018). Optimization of blood component production: A study on the effects of process changes.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That 